---
import WorksheetLayout from '../../../../layouts/WorksheetLayout.astro';
---
<WorksheetLayout
  title="Exhibition Plan"
  lesson="/courses/mfedu-2026/gallery-and-artist-statements/"
  intro="For teachers and student organizers: plan the gallery like a gallery, not a science fair."
>
  <h2>The shape of it</h2>
  <div class="ws-grid">
    <div>
      <p><strong>How many displays?</strong></p>
      <div class="ws-lines"><div></div></div>
      <p><strong>One-time event or temporary exhibit?</strong></p>
      <div class="ws-lines"><div></div></div>
      <p><strong>Runway show attached?</strong></p>
      <div class="ws-lines"><div></div></div>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p><strong>Location:</strong></p>
      <div class="ws-lines"><div></div></div>
      <p class="ws-hint">Cafeteria, library, classroom, hallway, community space, or a virtual gallery</p>
      <p><strong>Date &amp; setup time:</strong></p>
      <div class="ws-lines"><div></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Each display includes</h2>
  <ul class="ws-checks">
    <li>Design sketches (the process is as important as the product)</li>
    <li>Work-in-progress photos</li>
    <li>The finished piece — or a photo if it's being modeled</li>
    <li>The artist statement card</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Floor plan</h2>
  <p>Sketch the room: displays, walking path, power outlets, and lighting. Remember — the pieces glow, so where can the room go dim?</p>
  <div class="ws-box ws-box--tall"></div>

  <h2>Gallery rules of thumb</h2>
  <ul class="ws-checks">
    <li>Uniform poster/statement design so the gallery reads clean and professional</li>
    <li>Room between displays for the audience to move comfortably</li>
    <li>No "science fair" long-table lineups</li>
    <li>Designers and models have practiced answering questions</li>
  </ul>
</WorksheetLayout>
